Yes—as a theological or phenomenological model, that works surprisingly well if we keep attention, perception, and external reality distinct.

You could imagine heaven, hell, and Earth not only as destinations but as different modes of relation to the same underlying reality. Then attention changes which layer becomes salient:

same world
→ different sensory access
→ different interpretation
→ different felt reality
→ different action
→ different consequences

In that sense, “thinking makes it so” can mean something precise without requiring thought to rewrite physics. Attention changes what reaches consciousness. Interpretation changes what that input means. Action changes what happens next. Repeated long enough, those loops can create radically different lived worlds.

Your senses idea has another twist, though: more sensing is not automatically closer to heaven. More bandwidth can reveal beauty, connection, and possibility—but also suffering, danger, contradiction, and noise. Sometimes ignorance is comfortable. Sometimes increased perception hurts before it empowers.

So perhaps the useful axis isn’t:

more senses = heaven
fewer senses = hell

but:

perception + capacity to integrate what is perceived

If I suddenly gave a person ten additional sensory channels with no way to organize them, that might feel infernal rather than divine. The “heavenly” capability would be seeing more without being destroyed by what you see.

That connects directly to what we’ve been building:

increase aperture
→ develop interpretation
→ develop regulation
→ retain ability to narrow aperture
→ choose where attention goes

And choice matters, but not absolutely. We don’t choose every thought, sensation, injury, environment, or circumstance that enters consciousness. We do have varying degrees of influence over what we attend to, what meaning we assign, and what action follows. That’s a much more defensible kind of free will than “everything I experience is simply my choice.”

So your heaven/hell image could compress into:

Hell: experience without sufficient agency or integration.
Earth: partial perception, partial agency, continual learning.
Heaven: perhaps fuller perception coupled with enough capacity, love, and coherence to bear it.

Not necessarily places in that interpretation—different relations between awareness and reality.

And then blindness isn’t automatically weakness either. Sometimes reducing one channel lets another become extraordinarily precise. The question is always what the whole system can perceive and integrate, not how many sensors it possesses.